1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,632
Some plants flower only in winter.

2
00:00:02,632 --> 00:00:04,426
Some flower only in summer.

3
00:00:04,426 --> 00:00:08,118
And some seeds lie in the soil
for months without sprouting.

4
00:00:08,518 --> 00:00:10,631
They have no eyes and no calendar.

5
00:00:10,631 --> 00:00:12,520
So how do they know the time?

6
00:00:12,970 --> 00:00:15,297
Aarav noticed this on his bicycle road.

7
00:00:15,297 --> 00:00:19,000
The same bushes burst into flowers
in the same month every year.

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,700
He asked: how does a
plant know the season?

9
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:23,441
And why do some seeds wait?

10
00:00:23,791 --> 00:00:25,544
Here is the short answer.

11
00:00:25,544 --> 00:00:30,061
Plants measure the length of day and
night, some need a cold spell, and

12
00:00:30,061 --> 00:00:32,311
some seeds stay asleep on purpose.

13
00:00:32,811 --> 00:00:36,762
By the end, you will know photoperiodism,
vernalisation and seed dormancy,

14
00:00:36,762 --> 00:00:38,188
and how each one works.

15
00:00:38,838 --> 00:00:40,216
First, a quick note.

16
00:00:40,216 --> 00:00:44,601
This topic is not in the current NCERT
text, but it is in the ISC syllabus.

17
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:46,105
Photo means light.

18
00:00:46,105 --> 00:00:47,936
Period means a length of time.

19
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:51,867
Photoperiodism is how a plant responds
to the length of day and night.

20
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:56,333
Scientists found that the dark period
matters just as much as the light

21
00:00:56,333 --> 00:00:57,233
period.

22
00:00:56,938 --> 00:00:59,752
Together, they decide
when many plants flower.

23
00:01:00,402 --> 00:01:03,622
Let's zoom in and find where
the plant senses light.

24
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:05,553
Here is the whole plant.

25
00:01:05,553 --> 00:01:09,275
Flowers form at the shoot apex,
the growing tip of the shoot.

26
00:01:09,775 --> 00:01:13,025
But the shoot apex cannot
sense day length by itself.

27
00:01:13,025 --> 00:01:14,176
Zoom to a leaf.

28
00:01:14,176 --> 00:01:17,045
The leaves sense the
length of light and dark.

29
00:01:17,545 --> 00:01:18,960
Zoom into the leaf.

30
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:23,194
Scientists think a hormone-like signal
forms here, and travels from the

31
00:01:23,194 --> 00:01:26,838
leaf up to the shoot apex,
telling it to make flowers.

32
00:01:27,488 --> 00:01:31,690
Now follow one plant from day
length to a flower, step by step.

33
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:32,940
Step one.

34
00:01:32,729 --> 00:01:36,234
The leaves receive light and dark
for the right lengths of time.

35
00:01:36,984 --> 00:01:37,884
Step two.

36
00:01:37,631 --> 00:01:39,901
The leaves sense this
day and night length.

37
00:01:40,651 --> 00:01:41,551
Step three.

38
00:01:41,412 --> 00:01:43,579
A flowering signal forms in the leaves.

39
00:01:44,329 --> 00:01:45,229
Step four.

40
00:01:45,086 --> 00:01:47,460
The signal travels up to the shoot apex.

41
00:01:48,210 --> 00:01:49,110
Step five.

42
00:01:49,098 --> 00:01:53,242
The shoot apex turns into a
flowering apex, and flowers open.

43
00:01:53,992 --> 00:01:54,892
Remember this.

44
00:01:54,859 --> 00:01:56,188
Leaves sense the light.

45
00:01:56,188 --> 00:01:57,939
The shoot tip makes the flower.

46
00:01:58,539 --> 00:02:01,084
Plants fall into three
groups by day length.

47
00:02:01,434 --> 00:02:05,815
Long day plants need light for longer
than a critical duration, a fixed

48
00:02:05,815 --> 00:02:07,018
number of hours.

49
00:02:07,018 --> 00:02:11,136
Short day plants need light for
less than their critical duration.

50
00:02:11,586 --> 00:02:13,652
Day neutral plants do not care.

51
00:02:13,652 --> 00:02:16,454
Their flowering has no
link with day length.

52
00:02:17,104 --> 00:02:18,484
Now the second idea.

53
00:02:18,484 --> 00:02:21,244
Some plants need cold
before they can flower.

54
00:02:21,244 --> 00:02:25,468
This is vernalisation, flowering
helped by a period of low temperature.

55
00:02:25,818 --> 00:02:28,268
Wheat, barley and rye
have spring varieties.

56
00:02:28,268 --> 00:02:31,626
They are sown in spring and give
grain before the season ends.

57
00:02:32,076 --> 00:02:34,391
Their winter varieties are sown in autumn.

58
00:02:34,391 --> 00:02:38,179
They pass the winter as small
seedlings, grow again in spring, and are

59
00:02:38,179 --> 00:02:39,667
harvested around mid-summer.

60
00:02:40,317 --> 00:02:41,937
Why does this cold matter?

61
00:02:42,287 --> 00:02:46,040
Vernalisation stops a plant from
flowering too early, late in the growing

62
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:46,940
season.

63
00:02:46,589 --> 00:02:49,288
It gives the plant enough
time to grow up first.

64
00:02:49,738 --> 00:02:53,138
Biennial plants, which live
for two seasons, also use cold.

65
00:02:53,138 --> 00:02:56,445
Sugarbeet, cabbage and carrot
flower after a cold treatment.

66
00:02:57,045 --> 00:02:57,945
Your turn.

67
00:02:57,846 --> 00:03:00,168
A farmer sows winter wheat in spring.

68
00:03:00,168 --> 00:03:01,973
Will it give grain on time?

69
00:03:01,973 --> 00:03:03,153
Pause and decide.

70
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:07,253
No!

71
00:03:06,756 --> 00:03:10,837
It missed the cold it needs, so it
would normally fail to flower or make

72
00:03:10,837 --> 00:03:12,188
mature grain in time.

73
00:03:12,838 --> 00:03:14,906
Now the third idea, worked out.

74
00:03:14,906 --> 00:03:17,450
Why does a healthy seed refuse to sprout?

75
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,194
Some seeds do not germinate even
when water, air and warmth are fine.

76
00:03:22,194 --> 00:03:26,579
They are in dormancy, a resting state
controlled from inside the seed.

77
00:03:27,029 --> 00:03:27,929
The causes.

78
00:03:27,833 --> 00:03:30,219
A hard seed coat that water cannot pass.

79
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:33,173
Chemicals that block growth,
such as abscisic acid.

80
00:03:33,173 --> 00:03:35,384
Or an embryo that is not yet mature.

81
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:36,734
The fixes.

82
00:03:36,557 --> 00:03:38,953
Scratch the coat with
sandpaper or a knife.

83
00:03:38,953 --> 00:03:41,864
In nature, microbes and
the gut of animals do this.

84
00:03:41,864 --> 00:03:45,598
Chilling, gibberellic acid, nitrates,
light and temperature changes help

85
00:03:45,598 --> 00:03:46,498
too.

86
00:03:46,672 --> 00:03:48,134
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:52,292
Many students think the shoot tip
senses the day length, because flowers

88
00:03:52,292 --> 00:03:53,192
form there.

89
00:03:53,476 --> 00:03:56,118
But the leaves sense the
light and dark periods.

90
00:03:56,118 --> 00:03:58,461
The signal then travels to the shoot apex.

91
00:03:59,111 --> 00:04:00,325
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:00,325 --> 00:04:03,917
As statement or match questions on
the plant groups, vernalisation crops

93
00:04:03,917 --> 00:04:05,465
and ways to break dormancy.

94
00:04:06,065 --> 00:04:07,199
Exam level now.

95
00:04:07,199 --> 00:04:09,266
Which pair is matched correctly?

96
00:04:09,266 --> 00:04:10,449
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:15,849 --> 00:04:17,182
The answer is B.

98
00:04:17,182 --> 00:04:19,415
Winter wheat is sown in autumn.

99
00:04:19,915 --> 00:04:23,814
Short day plants need light for
less than the critical duration.

100
00:04:23,814 --> 00:04:27,561
And the leaves, not the shoot
apex, sense the photoperiod.

101
00:04:28,061 --> 00:04:29,877
Abscisic acid is an inhibitor.

102
00:04:29,877 --> 00:04:31,099
It causes dormancy.

103
00:04:31,099 --> 00:04:32,516
It does not break it.

104
00:04:33,116 --> 00:04:34,656
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:34,656 --> 00:04:36,239
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:39,439 --> 00:04:41,625
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:42,025 --> 00:04:42,925
One.

108
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:44,106
What is photoperiodism?

109
00:04:45,006 --> 00:04:45,906
Two.

110
00:04:45,275 --> 00:04:46,175
What is vernalisation?

111
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:47,880
Three.

112
00:04:47,526 --> 00:04:49,784
Name two ways to break seed dormancy.

113
00:04:50,884 --> 00:04:55,499
Next time: how does a fish breathe in
water, an insect through tiny tubes,

114
00:04:55,499 --> 00:04:57,245
and you through your nose?

115
00:04:57,245 --> 00:05:01,265
We find out in respiratory organs
and the human respiratory system.
